    {
      "kind": "statement",
      "eyebrow": "01 · The thesis",
      "title": "The technology is not the story. The cost line is.",
      "body": "Materials companies turn energy, ore and chemistry into specified inputs. Future demand is concentrated in a handful of new product families — battery materials, semiconductor-grade chemicals, rare earths and recycled feedstock — where qualification cycles are long and purity is the moat. Technology changes both the demand mix and the cost of producing it."
    },
    {
      "kind": "points",
      "eyebrow": "02 · Sector economics",
      "title": "What this sector actually sells",
      "body": "Revenue is volume times spread over raw material. Utilisation, energy cost and feedstock access decide margin, and qualification locks in customers for years.",
      "bullets": [
        "Commodity producers are price takers; specialty producers earn on specification and qualification.",
        "Energy is a large and volatile cost line in refining and smelting.",
        "Ore grade decline raises unit costs over time.",
        "Of take agreements and vertical integration secure demand for new materials.",
        "Permitting and environmental compliance set the pace of capacity additions."
      ]
    },
    {
